{
  "result": "There is a light that only exists between 5 and 6 in the morning. It isn't quite night, it isn't quite day. The world holds its breath. I love that hour, not because I am an early riser \u2014 I'm not \u2014 but because it feels borrowed, like time that doesn't count toward the rest of your life.\n\nIn that light, everything is softer. The sharp edges of yesterday's worries seem rounded. The email you forgot to send, the awkward thing you said at dinner, the bill on the fridge \u2014 they are still there, but they haven't woken up yet either. For a few minutes, you get to exist without being observed, even by yourself.\n\nI usually make coffee then. Not good coffee, necessarily. Instant coffee in a chipped mug that says 'World's Okayest Dad' even though I am not a dad. The kettle whistles, the steam fogs the window, and outside the street is empty except for a jogger and a cat who owns the block.\n\nI think we don't talk enough about how much of life is just these small, unremarkable rituals. We wait for big moments \u2014 graduations, weddings, breakthroughs \u2014 to give our lives meaning. But meaning, I've found, lives in the kettle, in the borrowed light, in the cat's blink.\n\nIf you get the chance, wake up early for no reason. Don't check your phone. Sit. Let the day come to you slowly. You might find it was waiting to be kinder than you expected.",
